1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
EdgeGuard: stop forklift accidents before they happen, with oneM2M and ETSI MEC.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:10,000
People and forklifts share aisles; proximity alarms are mostly false and do not say what to do.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:16,000
EdgeGuard predicts the collision 1 to 3 seconds ahead and tells each worker WAIT, STEP ASIDE or GO.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Each zone has a MEC host with an MN-CSE and the EdgeGuard MEC app; an IN-CSE in the cloud ties them together.

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:25,500
Live demo.

6
00:00:25,500 --> 00:01:25,500
Crossing, a worker in the lane, and two forklifts: the tag says WAIT, STEP ASIDE, then GO.

7
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:28,000
Firmware in the loop.

8
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:44,667
The ESP32 firmware runs unmodified against a real oneM2M CSE; a phone is the worker tag.

9
00:01:44,667 --> 00:01:47,167
Mobility via ETSI MEC.

10
00:01:47,167 --> 00:02:03,667
A MEC zone event moves the forklift's twin to zone B's MN-CSE: 1.5 to 8 ms interruption, no sample lost; RSRP of both cells cross at the boundary.

11
00:02:03,667 --> 00:02:13,667
All four TR-0077 clause 7 gaps run as EdgeGuard features, measured in CI on every commit.

12
00:02:13,667 --> 00:02:21,667
oneM2M does the work: SDT twins, group fan-out, pollingChannel, access control with TLS, announcement, and an AI agent over MCP.

13
00:02:21,667 --> 00:02:28,667
In simulation: collisions 100 to 4, throughput 61.7 to 86 percent, and 180 to 2 collisions during a network outage.

14
00:02:28,667 --> 00:02:33,667
EdgeGuard. Thank you.

